Complex-scope finding: the cross-worship network
This is the corpus's most significant complex-scope finding. In recognition of the high volume of non-Hindu visitors who come by from Kwan Im Thong Hood Cho Temple, the Sri Krishnan Temple placed a Kwan Im statue within their compounds and created a designated area for Chinese devotees to burn joss sticks. Sawasdee
This is a documented instance of the substitutes relation operating at complex scope and across religious traditions:
[SRI_KRISHNAN_KWAN_IM_STATUE  substitutes  KWAN_IM_THONG_STANDING_GUANYIN]
  [scope: complex]
  — the Sri Krishnan Hindu Temple has installed a Guanyin
    statue as a functional substitute for the Buddhist
    temple's principal deity, allowing Chinese devotees
    to continue their Guanyin devotion across the boundary
    of a Hindu temple compound

INSTITUTIONAL CONSEQUENCE:
  The two temples together constitute a functioning
  CROSS-RELIGIOUS COMPLEX in which the principal deity
  of a Chinese Buddhist temple has a designated substitute
  at an adjacent Hindu temple, and the two temples share
  a community of devotees who move between them.
